  "sources": [
    "style-research-extended-underground vol.5, scene-taxonomy addendum item 3 — `michigan-offgrid` (Rio Da Yung OG, Veeze, BabyTron; parent of Volume 4's `detroit-bounce`): BPM 92–115, straight, backbeat on 2&4, and four rules",
    "style-research-extended-underground vol.5 — Assignment note 2: the archetype is written as the PARENT of `detroit-bounce`; Rio Da Yung OG, Veeze and BabyTron are claimed there",
    "style-research-extended-4 vol.4 header — the live tempo discrepancy: ten Tee Grizzley readings split into a 92–104 straight cluster and a 67–83 halved cluster (134–150 half-time, snare on 3); carry BOTH as named modes",
    "style-research-extended-4 vol.4 detroit-bounce — reconciliation note against UNDERGROUND.md"
  ],
  "notes": "The Michigan lane's four rules, and the archetype volume 5 wrote as the parent of `detroit-bounce`. ⛔ **Rule 1 — the 808 does not slide. It plays discrete note figures**, which inverts drill's and trap's defining bass behaviour, so `bass808.slideProb` is **0** rather than merely low. `detroit-bounce` carries 0.04, which is the same idea stated softly; here it is the rule. ⛔ **Rule 2 — the hats are sparse: no 16th carpet, no triplet rolls.** `hihat.base` is eighths, `continuous` is false, `fillDensity` is 0.38 and `rolls.freqPerBar` is 0.05 with the vocabulary held to `\"16\"` — a triplet roll would be the one gesture the research names as absent. ⛔ **Rule 3 — the melodic figure is deliberately off the grid against a syncopated kick.** The kick carries `grammarVariants` (two-bar figures, the same device `detroit-bounce` uses) and the melody is the loosest part of the model. ⛔ **Rule 4 — nothing here is quantised tight.** The research says *\"the vocal is not quantised — stutters and takes' imperfections are retained by intent, so a humanise stage is required rather than optional\"*: `quantizeStrength` is 0.66, the loosest on the roster **except `jerk`**, whose 0.55 is its own signature and stays the floor. ⛔⛔ **BOTH TEMPO BANDS SHIP AS NAMED MODES, AND RECONCILING THEM WOULD BE THE ERROR.** Volume 5 specifies 92–115 straight with the backbeat on 2 and 4. Volume 4 measured ten Tee Grizzley readings and found a clean **92–104 straight** cluster *and* a **67–83 halved** cluster — 134–150 half-time with the snare on 3. Neither is wrong: Flint-independent material skews slow and straight, Detroit major-label skews fast and half-time. The roadmap's instruction is verbatim — *\"Author both as named modes; do not reconcile by picking one number\"* — so `flint` is the straight band and `detroit-halftime` is the halved one, and the invariant test asserts that the two modes really do land in different bands with different backbeats. ⚠ **`detroit-bounce` is NOT re-parented onto this, deliberately.** The research asks that it inherit rather than restate, and it currently extends `_defaults`; changing a shipped model's `extends` changes what every saved seed in every project reproduces, which is a decision with a migration attached rather than a tidy-up. Recorded for the next session. ⚠ **No chorus is a structural rule this engine cannot hold.** *\"Verses run continuously, so section lengths are long and irregular\"* — `arrangement.sectionBars` gives the long verse and a hook that is short enough to read as an interruption, which is as close as a section table gets. ⚠ **INFERRED, hence `medium`.** Sourced: both tempo bands, the backbeat placements, all four rules and the roster. Inferred: every velocity tier, every register, the key and scale lists, the `intervals`/`contour` split, the harmony (the research names none for this lane) and the mode names.",
